from . import n
# ---------- LEVEL 0 ----------
n("logic_sets","Logic and set theory","0",None,"C1","Mathematics",
 "Logic says which conclusions follow from which assumptions, and set theory (usually ZFC) gives a shared foundation in which nearly all of mathematics can be written down.",
 "A proof is a finite chain of steps from axioms by fixed inference rules. Because the foundations are chosen by convention, some statements (like the continuum hypothesis) turn out to be independent of ZFC.",
 "ZFC: Zermelo-Fraenkel axioms + choice; |ℕ| = ℵ₀ < |ℝ| = 2^ℵ₀ (Cantor). CH independent of ZFC (Gödel 1940, Cohen 1963).",
 "Pure formal reasoning; physics uses it as a language, not as evidence.",
 ev="Consistency relative to large-cardinal hierarchies|Independence proofs (forcing)",refs="CMA 1; CMA 2",ex="godel calculus probability")
n("godel","Gödel's incompleteness theorems","0",None,"C1","Mathematics",
 "Any consistent formal system strong enough to do arithmetic contains true statements it cannot prove. It also cannot prove its own consistency.",
 "The proof encodes statements about proofs as numbers, then builds a sentence that says 'I am not provable here'. This limits dreams of a single complete rulebook for mathematics.",
 "Theorem I: consistent, recursively axiomatized T ⊇ PA ⇒ ∃ G_T true but T ⊬ G_T. Theorem II: T ⊬ Con(T).",
 "Applies to formal systems that can express arithmetic; says nothing against everyday reasoning or physical theories that are not axiomatic in this sense.",
 em="logic_sets",ev="Rigorous proof (1931)",refs="CMA 2")
n("calculus","Calculus","0",None,"C1","Mathematics",
 "Calculus studies how quantities change (derivatives) and accumulate (integrals). It is the native language of motion, heat, waves and growth.",
 "The fundamental theorem links the two operations: integrating a rate gives a net change. Limits make the infinitesimal idea rigorous.",
 "f′(x) = lim_{h→0}[f(x+h)−f(x)]/h;  ∫ₐᵇ f′ dx = f(b)−f(a);  Taylor: f(x)=Σ f⁽ⁿ⁾(a)(x−a)ⁿ/n!",
 "Needs smooth-enough functions; discrete or fractal systems need other tools.",
 em="logic_sets",ev="Two centuries of successful physical prediction",refs="CA1 3; CA1 5; CA2 5",ex="ode_pde newton_laws")
n("ode_pde","Differential equations (ODEs and PDEs)","0",None,"C1","Mathematics",
 "A differential equation relates a quantity to its own rates of change. Ordinary ones describe a few moving parts; partial ones describe fields and fluids that vary in space and time.",
 "Most physical laws are of this form: given where a system is and how fast it is changing, the equation fixes the future. Few can be solved exactly, so numerical methods carry much of science.",
 "ODE: dy/dt = f(y,t). Heat: ∂u/∂t = α∇²u. Wave: ∂²u/∂t² = c²∇²u. Laplace: ∇²φ = 0.",
 "Existence and smoothness of solutions is not guaranteed for every nonlinear PDE (see Navier-Stokes).",
 em="calculus",ev="Numerical weather, orbit and circuit prediction",refs="CA2 4; CA3 7",ex="maxwell schrodinger navier_stokes heat_diffusion")
n("linalg_hilbert","Linear algebra and Hilbert spaces","0",None,"C1","Mathematics",
 "Vectors, matrices and linear maps give a way to handle many numbers at once. Quantum states live in a special vector space called a Hilbert space.",
 "Eigenvalues and eigenvectors pick out the special directions a transformation only stretches. Observables in quantum theory are operators whose eigenvalues are the possible results.",
 "Aψ = λψ; ⟨φ|ψ⟩ inner product; unitary U†U = 1 preserves probability; SVD: A = UΣV†.",
 "Linear structure is exact in quantum mechanics but only approximate for most macroscopic systems.",
 em="logic_sets",ev="Matrix mechanics reproduces atomic spectra",refs="UP3 7; CA3 2",ex="schrodinger neural_nets")
n("diff_geometry","Differential geometry","0",None,"C1","Mathematics",
 "Differential geometry describes curved spaces. It supplies the maths for general relativity, where gravity is the curvature of spacetime.",
 "Locally a curved space looks flat; the metric tells you how to measure distances and angles, and curvature measures how parallel lines drift apart.",
 "ds² = g_μν dx^μ dx^ν; Christoffel Γ^λ_μν = ½g^{λσ}(∂_μ g_σν+∂_ν g_σμ−∂_σ g_μν); Riemann R^ρ_σμν.",
 "Smooth manifolds only; quantum-scale spacetime may not be a smooth manifold.",
 em="calculus linalg_hilbert",ev="Underpins every GR test",refs="UP3 5",ex="general_relativity")
n("group_symmetry","Group theory and symmetry","0",None,"C1","Mathematics",
 "A symmetry is a change that leaves something looking the same. Group theory classifies all such changes and explains why particles and crystals come in neat patterns.",
 "Rotations, reflections and gauge transformations form groups. Particle families correspond to representations of groups such as SU(3), SU(2) and U(1).",
 "Standard Model gauge group SU(3)_C × SU(2)_L × U(1)_Y; 230 space groups classify crystals.",
 "Exact symmetries are rare; many are broken (e.g. electroweak).",
 em="logic_sets linalg_hilbert",ev="Eightfold way predicted the Ω⁻ baryon|Crystallographic classification",refs="UP3 11; UP3 9",ex="noether standard_model crystal_lattices symmetry_breaking")
n("noether","Noether's theorem","0",None,"C1","Mathematics",
 "Every continuous symmetry of a physical law brings a conserved quantity. Same laws at all times gives energy conservation, same laws everywhere gives momentum conservation, same laws in all directions gives angular momentum conservation.",
 "This explains why conservation laws exist rather than treating them as separate facts. Gauge symmetry brings conservation of electric charge.",
 "δS = 0 under q→q+εK ⇒ dQ/dt = 0, Q = Σ (∂L/∂q̇)K. Time shift → E; translation → p; rotation → L.",
 "Applies to systems with an action principle; energy is not globally conserved in an expanding universe.",
 em="group_symmetry lagrangian",ev="Underlies all verified conservation laws",refs="UP1 8; UP1 9",ex="conservation_energy conservation_momentum")
n("probability","Probability","0",None,"C1","Mathematics",
 "Probability measures how likely events are. It lets us reason about noise, chance, quantum outcomes and incomplete knowledge.",
 "Random variables have distributions with means and variances. The law of large numbers and the central limit theorem explain why averages are so well behaved.",
 "P(A|B)=P(A∩B)/P(B); E[X]=Σxp(x); Var X=E[X²]−E[X]²; Normal: p(x)=e^{−(x−μ)²/2σ²}/(σ√2π).",
 "Needs a well-defined sample space; subjective vs frequentist readings differ.",
 em="logic_sets",ev="Casino and insurance mathematics|Statistical physics",refs="IST 3; IST 4; IST 6",ex="bayes_stats statistical_mechanics")
n("bayes_stats","Bayesian inference and statistics","0",None,"C1","Mathematics",
 "Statistics turns data into conclusions with stated uncertainty. Bayes' rule says how to update beliefs when new evidence arrives.",
 "Sampling error shrinks as 1/√N, which is why more data narrows confidence intervals. Particle physics demands 5σ (about 1 in 3.5 million by chance) before announcing a discovery.",
 "P(H|D)=P(D|H)P(H)/P(D); CLT: x̄ ~ N(μ,σ²/N); 5σ ↔ p ≈ 2.9×10⁻⁷ (one-sided).",
 "Misuse (p-hacking, multiple comparisons) is a major source of unreliable claims.",
 em="probability",ev="Replication-crisis post-mortems",refs="IST 7; IST 8; IST 9; DSC 4",ex="scientific_method")
n("dynamical_chaos","Dynamical systems and chaos","0",None,"C1","Mathematics",
 "Simple rules can produce unpredictable behaviour. In chaotic systems, tiny differences in the starting state grow exponentially.",
 "Orbits settle on attractors; bifurcations are sudden qualitative changes as a parameter moves. The Lorenz equations, a toy weather model, show a butterfly-shaped strange attractor.",
 "Lyapunov exponent λ>0: δ(t)≈δ₀e^{λt}. Logistic map xₙ₊₁=r xₙ(1−xₙ), chaos for r≳3.57. Lorenz: ẋ=σ(y−x), ẏ=x(ρ−z)−y, ż=xy−βz.",
 "Deterministic but practically unpredictable beyond a Lyapunov time.",
 em="ode_pde",ev="Lorenz 1963|Double pendulum|Solar-system chaos over ~5 Myr",refs="CA2 4",ex="weather_prediction population_dynamics")
n("fourier","Fourier analysis","0",None,"C1","Mathematics",
 "Any signal can be built by adding simple sine waves. This explains musical tones, image compression and why waves of different colours can be separated.",
 "A function and its Fourier transform are two views of the same thing; narrow in one means broad in the other, which is the maths behind the uncertainty principle.",
 "f̂(k)=∫f(x)e^{−ikx}dx; Δx·Δk ≥ ½.",
 "Linear systems; nonlinear systems mix frequencies.",
 em="calculus linalg_hilbert",ev="Spectroscopy|MRI reconstruction|Audio codecs",refs="UP1 16; CA3 7",ex="uncertainty waves")
n("graph_theory","Graphs and networks","0",None,"C1","Mathematics",
 "A graph is dots joined by lines. It is the standard way to describe the internet, food webs, neurons and social ties.",
 "Key measures include degree, shortest paths and clustering. Many real networks are 'small-world' or have a few hugely connected hubs.",
 "Degree distribution P(k)∼k^{−γ} for scale-free nets (often 2<γ<3); Erdős-Rényi threshold ⟨k⟩=1.",
 "Real networks evolve, so static graph models are snapshots.",
 em="logic_sets",ev="Food web, protein-interaction and web-link studies",refs="CMA 12",ex="networks_complexity connectome")
n("millennium_problems","Open problems in mathematics","0",None,"C3","Mathematics",
 "Some of the deepest unanswered questions are in pure mathematics: the Riemann hypothesis, P versus NP, the Yang-Mills mass gap and whether fluid equations can blow up.",
 "Several of these control what physics and computing can do. The mass-gap problem asks for a rigorous proof of facts that experiments and computer lattice simulations already strongly suggest.",
 "Navier-Stokes: ∂ₜu+(u·∇)u=−∇p/ρ+ν∇²u, ∇·u=0 (smoothness for all time unproven in 3D unforced). RH: all nontrivial zeros of ζ(s) have Re s=½.",
 "Open as of the knowledge baseline; progress claims need peer review.",
 em="calculus ode_pde",refs="CMA 2",fr="Riemann hypothesis is unproven|Yang-Mills mass gap has no rigorous proof though lattice QCD supports it~open",fref="navier_stokes_ai",ex="p_vs_np")
# ---------- LEVEL 1 classical ----------
n("newton_laws","Newton's laws of motion","1",(1e-5,1e9),"C1","Classical mechanics",
 "An object keeps its velocity unless a force acts, acceleration equals force divided by mass, and forces come in equal opposite pairs. These rules explain everyday motion.",
 "Newton's second law, together with a force law like gravity, lets you compute future paths. Forces are tied to interactions, not to objects 'wanting' to move.",
 "F = dp/dt = ma; F₁₂ = −F₂₁; p = mv.",
 "Fails for v ≈ c (relativity), tiny scales (quantum), and very strong gravity (GR).",
 em="calculus noether",ev="Planetary and spacecraft trajectories|Lab mechanics",refs="UP1 5; UP1 6; HSP 4",
 ex="orbits_kepler projectile_motion fluid_statics rotational_dynamics",sim="human planet:earth")
n("gravitation_newton","Newtonian gravity","1",(1e-2,1e22),"C1","Classical mechanics",
 "Every mass attracts every other with a force that weakens with the square of the distance. It holds planets in orbit and makes things fall.",
 "Near Earth the pull is about 9.8 m/s² for everything, regardless of mass. Newton's law was the first law to unite earthly and heavenly motion.",
 "F = G m₁m₂/r², G = 6.674×10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻²; g = GM/R² ≈ 9.81 m/s²; v_esc = √(2GM/r).",
 "Breaks down near compact objects and at cosmological distances where general relativity matters.",
 em="newton_laws",ev="Cavendish experiment|Moon and planetary orbits",refs="UP1 13; AST 3; HSP 7",ex="orbits_kepler tides general_relativity",sim="solarsystem planet:earth moon")
n("orbits_kepler","Orbits and Kepler's laws","1",(1e6,1e16),"C1","Classical mechanics",
 "Planets move on ellipses with the Sun at one focus, sweep equal areas in equal times, and take longer to orbit the farther out they are.",
 "These laws follow from inverse-square gravity and angular momentum conservation. Orbital periods let astronomers weigh stars and planets.",
 "T² = 4π²a³/(G(M+m)); L = mr²θ̇ constant; vis-viva v² = GM(2/r−1/a).",
 "Small corrections from other bodies and relativity (Mercury's 43″/century perihelion advance).",
 em="gravitation_newton conservation_momentum",ev="Planetary ephemerides|Exoplanet radial velocities",refs="AST 3; UP1 13",sim="solarsystem planet:mercury")
n("conservation_energy","Conservation of energy","1",None,"C1","Classical mechanics",
 "Energy changes form (motion, height, heat, light) but its total in an isolated system never changes. It is one of the most reliable rules in science.",
 "Work done by forces moves energy between kinetic and potential forms; friction turns it into heat. Noether's theorem ties it to the laws being the same at all times.",
 "E = ½mv² + U; W = ∫F·dr; ΔKE = W_net; 1 J = 1 kg m² s⁻².",
 "Locally exact; in an expanding universe a global energy is not well defined.",
 em="newton_laws noether",ev="Every calorimetry and collision experiment|Neutrino hypothesis rescued it in β decay",refs="UP1 7; UP1 8; HSP 9",ex="first_law heat_engines")
n("conservation_momentum","Momentum and collisions","1",None,"C1","Classical mechanics",
 "When objects collide or explode, their combined momentum (mass times velocity) is unchanged. Rockets and billiard balls both obey this.",
 "Elastic collisions also conserve kinetic energy; inelastic ones lose some to heat or deformation. Impulse is force acting over time.",
 "Σp_before = Σp_after; J = ∫F dt = Δp; rocket: Δv = v_e ln(m₀/m₁).",
 "Exact in an isolated system at all scales (Noether), extended to p = γmv for relativity.",
 em="newton_laws noether",ev="Collision experiments from labs to accelerators",refs="UP1 9; HSP 8",ex="rocketry")
n("rotational_dynamics","Rotation and angular momentum","1",(1e-3,1e9),"C1","Classical mechanics",
 "Spinning things resist changes to their spin. Pull your arms in and you spin faster because angular momentum is conserved.",
 "Torque changes angular momentum like force changes momentum. Gyroscopes, spinning planets and collapsing stars all use this.",
 "τ = dL/dt; L = Iω; I = Σmr²; KE_rot = ½Iω².",
 "Relativistic and quantum versions add spin; classical formula fails for fundamental spin-½ particles.",
 em="newton_laws noether",ev="Figure-skater spin-up|Pulsar spin periods",refs="UP1 10; UP1 11",sim="planet:earth")
n("lagrangian","Lagrangian and Hamiltonian mechanics","1",None,"C1","Classical mechanics",
 "Instead of tracking forces, you can find the motion that makes a quantity called action stationary. This viewpoint generalises easily to fields and quantum theory.",
 "Energy-based formulations make constraints and symmetries easy to handle. The Hamiltonian version is the direct ancestor of the Schrödinger equation.",
 "S=∫L dt, L=T−V; d/dt(∂L/∂q̇)=∂L/∂q; H=Σpq̇−L; q̇=∂H/∂p, ṗ=−∂H/∂q.",
 "Classical; the path-integral generalises it to quantum theory.",
 em="calculus newton_laws",ev="Equivalent to Newton for conservative systems",refs="UP1 8",ex="schrodinger noether")
n("oscillations","Oscillations and resonance","1",(1e-10,1e3),"C1","Classical mechanics",
 "Anything with a restoring force near equilibrium swings back and forth. Pushing at its natural frequency builds large oscillations (resonance).",
 "Springs, pendulums, molecular bonds and electric circuits all share the same maths. Damping makes the swing die out.",
 "ẍ+ω₀²x=0, ω₀=√(k/m); damped driven: ẍ+2γẋ+ω₀²x=F cosΩt/m; Q=ω₀/2γ.",
 "Linear only for small amplitudes.",
 em="newton_laws ode_pde",ev="Tacoma Narrows bridge|Quartz clocks",refs="UP1 15",ex="waves")
n("waves","Waves and sound","1",(1e-10,1e7),"C1","Classical mechanics",
 "A wave carries energy and information without carrying matter along. Sound, water ripples and seismic shaking are all waves.",
 "Waves interfere, reflect, refract and diffract. Speed depends on the medium: stiffer and lighter means faster.",
 "v=fλ; v_string=√(T/μ); v_sound,air≈331+0.6T(°C) m/s ≈ 343 m/s at 20 °C; I∝A²; Doppler f′=f(v±v_o)/(v∓v_s).",
 "Linear superposition holds for small amplitude; shocks form when it fails.",
 em="oscillations ode_pde",ev="Ripple tank|Seismology|Ultrasound imaging",refs="UP1 16; UP1 17; HSP 13",ex="seismology sound_hearing",sim="air")
n("elasticity_materials","Stress, strain and elasticity","1",(1e-9,1e2),"C1","Classical mechanics",
 "Solids stretch in proportion to the force until they permanently deform or break. Stiffness comes from atomic bonds acting like tiny springs.",
 "Young's modulus tells how stiff a material is. Beyond the elastic limit, defects move and the material yields.",
 "σ=Eε; E_steel≈200 GPa, E_rubber≈0.01–0.1 GPa; shear τ=Gγ; bulk B=−V dP/dV.",
 "Linear elasticity valid for small strains; plasticity and fracture need other models.",
 em="newton_laws electrostatic_bonds",ev="Hooke's law tests|Bridge and aircraft engineering",refs="UP1 12",ex="dislocations_strength",sim="rock")
n("electrostatic_bonds","Atomic-scale springs (interatomic potentials)","4",(1e-10,1e-9),"C1","Chemistry",
 "Atoms attract at moderate distances and repel when too close, so the energy has a minimum at the bond length. Near that minimum the bond acts like a spring.",
 "The Lennard-Jones form is a common model of this balance. Such curves explain thermal expansion, stiffness and melting.",
 "V(r)=4ε[(σ/r)¹²−(σ/r)⁶]; minimum at r=2^{1/6}σ; k=V″(r₀).",
 "Classical approximation for neutral atoms; real bonds need quantum mechanics.",
 em="coulomb_law quantum_orbitals",ev="Spectroscopy of diatomic molecules",refs="CHE 7; CHE 10",ex="elasticity_materials")
# fluids
n("fluid_statics","Pressure, buoyancy and Archimedes","1",(1e-3,1e7),"C1","Fluids",
 "Pressure in a fluid grows with depth, and an object feels an upward push equal to the weight of fluid it displaces. That is why ships float and divers feel pressure.",
 "Atmospheric pressure at sea level (≈101 kPa) is the weight of the air column above. Pascal's principle underlies hydraulic machines.",
 "P=P₀+ρgh; F_b=ρ_fluid V g; 1 atm = 101 325 Pa.",
 "Incompressible, static fluid assumed; ρ varies in gases.",
 em="newton_laws",ev="Barometers|Submarine design",refs="UP1 14",ex="bernoulli_flight",sim="liquid:water air")
n("bernoulli_flight","Bernoulli's principle and lift","1",(1e-3,1e2),"C1","Fluids",
 "In smoothly flowing fluid, faster flow goes with lower pressure. Wings turn the air downward, and that and the pressure difference produce lift.",
 "Newton's third law and Bernoulli's equation are two descriptions of the same momentum change. Stall happens when flow separates from the wing.",
 "P+½ρv²+ρgh=const along a streamline; L=½ρv²SC_L.",
 "Steady, incompressible, inviscid flow; fails with strong viscosity or shocks.",
 em="fluid_statics conservation_energy",ev="Wind tunnel tests|Aviation",refs="UP1 14",ex="flight_tech",sim="air bird")
n("navier_stokes","Navier-Stokes equations","1",(1e-6,1e8),"C1","Fluids",
 "These equations say how a fluid's velocity changes because of pressure, viscosity and external forces. They govern air, water, blood and the weather.",
 "Solutions are well tested, but whether they stay smooth forever in 3D is a famous unsolved problem. Practical simulations use numerical grids and turbulence models.",
 "ρ(∂ₜu+u·∇u)=−∇p+μ∇²u+f; ∇·u=0; Re=ρUL/μ.",
 "Continuum limit (length ≫ mean free path); non-Newtonian fluids need modified stress laws.",
 em="newton_laws ode_pde",ev="Pipe flow, aerodynamics and ocean models",refs="UP1 14",ex="turbulence weather_dynamics blood_circulation",fref="navier_stokes_ai",sim="air liquid:water")
n("turbulence","Turbulence","1",(1e-4,1e7),"C3","Fluids",
 "Fast flows break into swirling eddies of all sizes. Turbulence mixes heat and momentum very efficiently but has no complete first-principles theory.",
 "Energy cascades from big eddies to small ones until viscosity turns it into heat. Kolmogorov's 1941 scaling predicts how the energy is spread over eddy sizes.",
 "E(k)=C ε^{2/3}k^{−5/3}, C≈1.5; Kolmogorov scale η=(ν³/ε)^{1/4}; Re_crit(pipe)≈2300.",
 "Statistical scaling is well supported; exact closure is unsolved.",
 em="navier_stokes dynamical_chaos",ev="Wind-tunnel and ocean turbulence spectra",refs="UP1 14",ex="weather_dynamics",fr="No first-principles derivation of turbulence statistics~open",sim="cloud")
# thermodynamics
n("temperature_heat","Temperature and heat","1",(1e-9,1e7),"C1","Thermodynamics",
 "Temperature measures the average random motion energy of particles; heat is energy moving because of a temperature difference.",
 "Heat flows by conduction, convection and radiation. Materials differ in how much heat they need to warm up (specific heat).",
 "Q=mcΔT; q=−k∇T; P_rad=εσAT⁴, σ=5.670×10⁻⁸ W m⁻² K⁻⁴; Wien λ_max T=2.898×10⁻³ m K.",
 "Local equilibrium assumed; temperature is undefined far from equilibrium.",
 em="statistical_mechanics",ev="Calorimetry|Thermal imaging",refs="UP2 1; HSP 11",ex="heat_diffusion greenhouse_effect blackbody",sim="liquid:water")
n("heat_diffusion","Heat conduction and diffusion","1",(1e-9,1e5),"C1","Thermodynamics",
 "Heat and dissolved particles spread out from hot or concentrated regions to cooler or emptier ones, driven by random motion.",
 "The same equation describes cooling coffee, ink in water and the slow flow of heat through Earth's crust. The spread grows as the square root of time.",
 "∂u/∂t=D∇²u; ⟨x²⟩=2Dt; Fick J=−D∇c.",
 "Valid when transport is diffusive, not ballistic.",
 em="ode_pde temperature_heat",ev="Brownian motion (Perrin)|Geothermal gradients",refs="UP2 1; BIO 5",ex="membrane_transport")
n("kinetic_theory","Kinetic theory of gases","1",(1e-10,1e1),"C1","Thermodynamics",
 "Gas pressure comes from countless molecules bouncing off walls. Temperature is a measure of how energetic those motions are.",
 "The ideal-gas law follows from this picture. Real gases deviate when molecules attract or take up space.",
 "PV=NkT=nRT; ⟨½mv²⟩=3/2 kT; v_rms=√(3kT/m); R=8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹; k=1.380649×10⁻²³ J/K.",
 "Dilute, high-temperature limit; van der Waals corrections otherwise.",
 em="newton_laws statistical_mechanics",ev="Gas laws|Brownian motion",refs="UP2 2; CHE 9",ex="gas_air_properties",sim="gas:air")
n("first_law","First law of thermodynamics","1",None,"C1","Thermodynamics",
 "Energy is conserved when heat and work are both counted: heat added to a system either warms it or lets it do work.",
 "It forbids machines that output more energy than they take in. Internal energy is stored in the random motion and interactions of particles.",
 "ΔU=Q−W; dU=TdS−PdV; C_p−C_v=R (ideal gas).",
 "Universal for macroscopic systems.",
 em="conservation_energy",ev="Joule's paddle-wheel experiment",refs="UP2 3; CHE 5",ex="heat_engines")
n("second_law","Second law and entropy","X",None,"C1","Thermodynamics",
 "In an isolated system disorder, measured as entropy, tends to increase. This gives time its arrow and explains why heat flows from hot to cold.",
 "Entropy counts how many microscopic arrangements match what we see. Local order (like life) is possible only by exporting entropy.",
 "dS≥δQ/T; S=k_B ln W (k_B=1.380649×10⁻²³ J/K); η_Carnot=1−T_c/T_h.",
 "Statistical law, essentially exact for macroscopic systems; fluctuation theorems cover tiny systems.",
 em="statistical_mechanics",ev="Never-observed violations|Carnot engine limits",refs="UP2 4; CHE 16",ex="heat_engines arrow_of_time chemistry_gibbs",fr="Why the early universe had very low entropy is unexplained~open")
n("third_law","Third law and absolute zero","1",None,"C1","Thermodynamics",
 "You cannot reach absolute zero in a finite number of steps. Entropy of a perfect crystal approaches zero at 0 K.",
 "Cooling gets harder near zero because there is less heat to extract. Quantum effects like superfluidity appear at very low temperature.",
 "S→0 as T→0 (perfect crystal); 0 K=−273.15 °C.",
 "Residual entropy exists for disordered solids like ice.",
 em="second_law quantum_orbitals",ev="Cryogenic experiments approaching nanokelvin",refs="CHE 16")
n("statistical_mechanics","Statistical mechanics","1",(1e-10,1e3),"C1","Thermodynamics",
 "Statistical mechanics derives the laws of heat from the random motion of huge numbers of atoms. It is the main bridge from microscopic to macroscopic.",
 "At equilibrium, a state with energy E is occupied with probability proportional to e^(−E/kT). Averaging over this distribution gives pressure, heat capacity and magnetisation.",
 "p_i=e^{−E_i/kT}/Z; Z=Σe^{−E_i/kT}; F=−kT ln Z; S=−k Σp ln p.",
 "Needs many particles and ergodic mixing; small or driven systems need fluctuation theorems.",
 em="probability newton_laws",ev="Heat capacities|Blackbody spectrum|Magnetic transitions",refs="UP2 2; UP2 4",ex="kinetic_theory second_law phase_transitions chemistry_equilibrium")
n("heat_engines","Heat engines and refrigerators","7",(1e-2,1e2),"C1","Engineering",
 "Engines turn heat into work by moving heat from hot to cold, and the second law caps their efficiency. Refrigerators run the same process in reverse using work.",
 "Real car engines (Otto cycle) and power plants (Rankine cycle) reach well below the Carnot limit because of friction and finite-speed heat flow.",
 "η≤1−T_c/T_h; Otto η=1−r^{1−γ}; COP_fridge=T_c/(T_h−T_c).",
 "Cycle models idealise combustion; irreversibility cuts real efficiency.",
 em="first_law second_law",ev="Power-plant efficiencies 30–60 %",refs="UP2 4; UP2 3",ex="power_grid")
n("phase_transitions","Phase transitions and critical phenomena","1",(1e-10,1e3),"C1","Thermodynamics",
 "Matter changes phase (melting, boiling, magnetising) abruptly when conditions cross a threshold. Near critical points many different systems behave in the same universal way.",
 "Phase transitions are collective effects: no single molecule 'melts'. Universality means details drop out near a critical point.",
 "Clausius-Clapeyron dP/dT=L/(TΔv); order parameter ∼|T−T_c|^β; 3D Ising β≈0.326.",
 "Mean-field theory fails near T_c in low dimension.",
 em="statistical_mechanics",ev="Critical opalescence|Ferromagnet Curie point",refs="UP2 1; CHE 10",ex="symmetry_breaking liquid_water_properties",sim="liquid:water")
n("blackbody","Thermal radiation and blackbody spectrum","1",(1e-7,1e-3),"C1","Thermodynamics",
 "Hot objects glow, and the colour depends only on temperature. This is why a heated metal goes red then white and why stars have colours.",
 "Planck explained the spectrum by assuming light energy comes in packets, starting quantum theory. The Sun's surface (5772 K) peaks in the visible.",
 "B_λ=(2hc²/λ⁵)/(e^{hc/λkT}−1); h=6.62607×10⁻³⁴ J s; L=4πR²σT⁴.",
 "Ideal for opaque bodies; real objects have emissivity<1.",
 em="statistical_mechanics electromagnetic_waves",ev="COBE FIRAS CMB spectrum|Stellar colours",refs="UP3 6; AST 5",ex="quantum_discovery stellar_spectra cmb")
# ---------- EM ----------
n("coulomb_law","Electric charge and Coulomb's law","1",(1e-15,1e3),"C1","Electromagnetism",
 "Like charges repel and opposite charges attract, with a force that weakens with distance squared. This force holds atoms and molecules together.",
 "The electric force between a proton and electron is about 10³⁹ times stronger than their gravity. Charge is quantised in units of e.",
 "F=k q₁q₂/r², k=8.988×10⁹ N m²/C²; e=1.602176634×10⁻¹⁹ C; ε₀=8.854×10⁻¹² F/m.",
 "Static point charges; moving charges need full electrodynamics.",
 em="qed",ev="Torsion-balance tests|Atomic structure",refs="UP2 5; HSP 18",ex="electric_fields electrostatic_bonds chemical_bonding")
n("electric_fields","Electric fields, potential and capacitance","1",(1e-9,1e4),"C1","Electromagnetism",
 "A charge sets up an electric field that pushes other charges. Voltage is the energy per unit charge, and capacitors store charge by holding a field between plates.",
 "Gauss's law relates field flux to enclosed charge. Conductors in equilibrium have no internal field, shielding what is inside.",
 "E=F/q; ∮E·dA=Q/ε₀; V=−∫E·dl; C=Q/V=ε₀A/d; U=½CV².",
 "Classical fields; quantised at the photon level.",
 em="coulomb_law",ev="Faraday cage|Capacitor measurements",refs="UP2 6; UP2 7; UP2 8",ex="circuits_ohm maxwell")
n("circuits_ohm","Circuits, Ohm's law and Kirchhoff's rules","7",(1e-6,1e3),"C1","Electromagnetism",
 "Current is charge flow, and in many materials it is proportional to the voltage across them. Circuit rules let you predict how currents split and voltages drop.",
 "Resistance comes from electrons scattering off lattice vibrations and defects. Power dissipated as heat is voltage times current.",
 "V=IR; P=IV=I²R; Kirchhoff ΣI=0 at nodes, ΣV=0 around loops; RC time constant τ=RC.",
 "Ohmic materials; diodes and transistors are nonlinear.",
 em="electric_fields band_theory",ev="Everyday electronics",refs="UP2 9; UP2 10",ex="electronics_logic power_grid")
n("magnetism_force","Magnetic fields and forces","1",(1e-15,1e7),"C1","Electromagnetism",
 "Moving charges feel a sideways force in a magnetic field, and moving charges also make magnetic fields. Compass needles, motors and particle accelerators rely on this.",
 "Magnetism is a relativistic companion of electricity: what is magnetic in one frame partly looks electric in another. Permanent magnets come from aligned electron spins.",
 "F=q(E+v×B); r=mv/(qB); ∮B·dl=μ₀I; μ₀≈1.2566×10⁻⁶ N/A².",
 "Classical; single-spin magnetism is quantum.",
 em="coulomb_law special_relativity",ev="Cyclotron motion|Magnetic field of Earth",refs="UP2 11; UP2 12",ex="induction_generators magnetic_materials geodynamo")
n("induction_generators","Electromagnetic induction","1",(1e-6,1e3),"C1","Electromagnetism",
 "A changing magnetic field creates an electric field. This is how generators produce electricity and transformers change voltage.",
 "Lenz's law says the induced current opposes the change that caused it, which is energy conservation in action.",
 "ε=−dΦ_B/dt; transformer V_s/V_p=N_s/N_p; L: ε=−L dI/dt; X_L=ωL.",
 "Classical; exact within Maxwell theory.",
 em="magnetism_force maxwell",ev="Faraday 1831|All power stations",refs="UP2 13; UP2 14; UP2 15",ex="power_grid electric_motors")
n("maxwell","Maxwell's equations","1",None,"C1","Electromagnetism",
 "Four equations describe all classical electric and magnetic phenomena, and show that light is an electromagnetic wave.",
 "They unified electricity, magnetism and optics in 1865 and led to special relativity. The speed of the waves comes out from constants measured in the lab.",
 "∇·E=ρ/ε₀; ∇·B=0; ∇×E=−∂B/∂t; ∇×B=μ₀J+μ₀ε₀∂E/∂t; c=1/√(μ₀ε₀)=299 792 458 m/s.",
 "Classical; QED supersedes it for single photons and strong fields.",
 em="electric_fields magnetism_force induction_generators",ev="Hertz's radio waves|Every radio and optical device",refs="UP2 16",ex="electromagnetic_waves special_relativity qed")
n("electromagnetic_waves","Electromagnetic waves and spectrum","1",(1e-14,1e5),"C1","Electromagnetism",
 "Light, radio, microwaves, X-rays and gamma rays are the same kind of wave differing only in wavelength. They carry energy through empty space at the speed of light.",
 "The visible band is a sliver between 380 and 750 nm. Photon energy rises as wavelength falls, so gamma rays ionise atoms while radio waves do not.",
 "c=fλ; E_photon=hf=hc/λ ≈ 1240 eV nm/λ; Poynting S=E×B/μ₀.",
 "Wave picture for many photons; particle picture for single events.",
 em="maxwell",ev="Radio astronomy to gamma-ray astronomy",refs="UP2 16; AST 5; UP3 1",ex="optics_geometric blackbody stellar_spectra radio_comm greenhouse_effect")
n("optics_geometric","Geometric optics and lenses","1",(1e-6,1e2),"C1","Optics",
 "Light rays bend when entering glass or water, which lets lenses form images in cameras, eyes and telescopes.",
 "Refraction follows Snell's law; total internal reflection traps light in optical fibre. Aberrations limit simple lenses.",
 "n₁sinθ₁=n₂sinθ₂; 1/f=1/d_o+1/d_i; lensmaker 1/f=(n−1)(1/R₁−1/R₂); n_water=1.33, n_glass≈1.5.",
 "Valid when features ≫ wavelength.",
 em="electromagnetic_waves",ev="Telescopes and microscopes",refs="UP3 1; UP3 2",ex="telescopes_tech vision_eye fiber_optics")
n("wave_optics","Interference, diffraction and polarisation","1",(1e-9,1e-2),"C1","Optics",
 "Light waves add and cancel to make bright and dark fringes, and spread around obstacles. This shows light's wave nature and sets the limit on how fine an image can be.",
 "Slit patterns reveal wavelength; thin films and soap bubbles show colour from interference. Polarisation selects the wave's oscillation direction.",
 "d sinθ=mλ; single slit a sinθ=mλ; Rayleigh limit θ=1.22λ/D; Malus I=I₀cos²θ.",
 "Scalar/Maxwell wave theory; needs quantum treatment for single-photon statistics.",
 em="electromagnetic_waves",ev="Young's double slit|Michelson interferometer|LIGO",refs="UP3 3; UP3 4",ex="telescopes_tech wave_particle")
